  <HOMEWORK title="Homework 26: It's locked. It's also wrong." prompt="A frequency synthesizer is supposed to multiply a 25 MHz reference up to 1000 MHz. Lab status shows LOCKED, steady, no alarms. A technician downstream reports every measurement off this clock is subtly wrong. Before assuming their equipment is miscalibrated, check what this synthesizer is actually putting out."
            hint="Read the VCO output frequency directly, not just the lock indicator. Does it match 25 MHz x the programmed feedback divider?"
            explanation="The feedback divider is programmed for N=41, not the intended N=40. The loop does exactly what it's designed to do - it locks the VCO to N times the reference - it just locks to the wrong N. A PLL's lock detector only confirms the loop found a stable, phase-aligned state; it says nothing about whether that state is the one you actually wanted. This is a real, easy-to-make bring-up mistake (a register field one off, a strapping error) that produces zero alarms and a perfectly confident LOCKED status - always cross-check the actual output frequency against reference x N, not just the lock flag, before trusting a synthesizer's output."/>
</LABCHAIN>
